README for X3P Scan Viewer
Copyright (c) 2026 Oliver Kreylos
========================================================================

Overview
========

The X3P Scan Viewer is an interactive visual utility to extract
microstamping tags from 3D scans of spent bullet casings. The X3P Scan
Viewer is based on the Vrui software development toolkit (see
https://github.com/vrui-vr/vrui), and runs in a variety of environments
ranging from laptop or desktop computers over semi-immersive
environments such as Geowalls to fully immersive virtual reality
environments such as CAVEs or head-mounted VR displays. More information
about the X3P Scan Viewer can be found at
https://github.com/vrui-vr/x3pviewer.

Requirements
============

The X3P Scan Viewer requires Vrui version 15.0 build 001 or newer.

Installation Guide
==================

It is recommended to download or move the source packages for Vrui and
the X3P Scan Viewer into a src directory underneath the user's home
directory. Otherwise, references to ~/src in the following instructions
need to be changed.

It is also recommended to skip optional steps 4 and 6 in the following
instructions. The X3P Scan Viewer does not need to be installed in order
to be used; installation (to a system directory such as /usr/local) is
only recommended if the X3P Scan Viewer will be used from multiple user
accounts.

0.1. Install Vrui from ~/src/Vrui-<version>-<build> (see Vrui README file).

1. Change into ~/src directory and unpack the X3P Scan Viewer tarball:
   > cd ~/src
   > tar xfz <download path>/X3PViewer-<version>.tar.gz
   - or -
   > tar xf <download path>/X3PViewer-<version>.tar

2. Change into the X3P Scan Viewer base directory:
   > cd X3PViewer-<version>

3. If the Vrui version installed in step 0 was not 15.0, or Vrui's
   installation directory was changed from the default of /usr/local,
   adapt the makefile using a text editor. Change the value of
   VRUI_MAKEDIR close to the beginning of the file as follows:
   VRUI_MAKEDIR := <Vrui install dir>/share/make
   Where <Vrui install dir> is the installation directory chosen in
   step 0. Use $(HOME) to refer to the user's home directory instead
   of ~.

4. Optional: Adapt makefile if the X3P Scan Viewer is to be installed in
   a different location, for example /usr/local. Set INSTALLDIR to the
   desired target location. The X3P Scan Viewer will then be installed
   in <INSTALLDIR>/bin, and shaders will be installed in
   <INSTALLDIR>/share (where <INSTALLDIR> is the value of INSTALLDIR set
   in the makefile).

5. Build the X3P Scan Viewer:
   > make
   This creates the X3PViewer executable in ./bin.

6. Optional: Install the X3P Scan Viewer in the selected target
   location. This is only necessary if the INSTALLDIR variable in the
   makefile was changed. By default, the X3P Scan Viewer can be run from
   its base directory. To install:
   > make install
   - or, if the target location is a system directory -
   > sudo make install
   This will copy all executables into <INSTALLDIR>/bin, and the shader
   files into <INSTALLDIR>/share.

7. Optional: Add directory containing the X3P Scan Viewer executable (in
   ~/src/X3PViewer-<version>/bin in the default installation, in
   <INSTALLDIR>/bin otherwise) to the user's search path. This allows
   running the X3P Scan Viewer from any directory. Using csh
   or tcsh:
   > setenv PATH ${PATH}:~/src/X3PViewer-<version>/bin
   - or -
   > setenv PATH ${PATH}:<INSTALLDIR>/bin
   where <INSTALLDIR> is the target location set in the makefile.
   Using bash:
   > export PATH=${PATH}:~/src/X3PViewer-<version>/bin
   - or -
   > export PATH=${PATH}:<INSTALLDIR>/bin
   These lines can also be added to the user's .cshrc or .bashrc files
   to make the additions persist between logins.

Running the X3P Scan Viewer
===========================

These instructions assume that the X3P Scan Viewer was installed in its
base directory, (see steps 4 and 6 above).

1. On MacOS X: Start X11. The X3P Scan Viewer requires a running X
   server, but, if preferred, it can be started from a MacOS X terminal
   instead of xterm.

2. See Vrui's HTML documentation on Vrui's basic user interface and how
   to use the X3P Scan Viewer.
